<decompilertest>
<!--
    kuna-revisitrefine: refining a range a later heritage pass revisited.

    Issue #1034: decompile-all of a large Rust/MSVC DLL panicked in
    Heritage::build_refinement ("index out of bounds: the len is 8 but the
    index is 8").

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 heritage, phi-range refinement of a revisited range
    (strict bug fix, no option).

    The first stack pass heritages the 8-byte slots at rsp+0x10 and rsp+0x18
    apart and places a MULTIEQUAL for each at the join. The 8-byte read at
    rsp+0x13 goes through a spilled pointer, so it is seen only on the second
    pass. It joins both slots into one 16-byte range, which is refined at
    0, 3, 8 and 11. Each old MULTIEQUAL spans two partitions; they were left
    to the partitions, so each 3-byte partition held an 8-byte phi: the read's
    high piece was linked to the whole 8-byte phi of rsp+0x18 and the return
    printed CONCAT85, a 13-byte value in an 8-byte sum. A partition that also
    takes a frame byte copy is refined again, and that refinement indexed past
    its array.

    Assembled with clang -c (x86-64), the .text bytes of f verbatim below:

        f:  sub rsp,0x38
            mov [rsp+0x10],rdi
            mov [rsp+0x18],rsi
            test edx,edx
            je .L1
            mov qword [rsp+0x10],1
            mov qword [rsp+0x18],2
        .L1:
            lea rax,[rsp+0x13]
            mov [rsp+0x8],rax
            mov rcx,[rsp+0x8]
            mov rax,[rcx]
            add rax,[rsp+0x10]
            add rax,[rsp+0x18]
            add rsp,0x38
            ret

    #1: the unaligned read is the 3 low bytes of rsp+0x18 over the 5 high
    bytes of rsp+0x10 (the old build printed CONCAT85).  #2: both slots are
    one 8-byte variable each.
-->
